 \cortext[cor1]{Corresponding author: Yunfei Yin (yunfeiyin@hit.edu.cn).}
 
 \begin{abstract}
-Large language models (LLMs) can describe how travelers might react to delays, fares or rain, but querying them for every agent in a city-scale simulation remains costly. Distilling their judgments into compact traveler models removes this cost and raises a question that predictive accuracy does not answer, namely whether the distilled model changes its choices in the same way as its source when conditions change. This study evaluates LLM-derived traveler models by their responses to paired changes in travel conditions and follows these responses through comparisons with the LLM Teacher, with stated choices and with simulated network demand. On a held-out benchmark of six synthetic personas and three training seeds, paired response supervision lowers probability-response error by 4.97\% relative to pointwise distribution matching, and a multinomial logit Student lowers it by a further 14.30\%. Combining that choice model with an independent neural departure predictor gives a departure error of 7.61 minutes against the Teacher, within the range of jointly trained neural models. Stated-choice data from 332 Singapore and 321 Shanghai respondents show that agreement with people depends on the intervention, and the model with the smallest Teacher error reverses the stated response to poorer public-transport accessibility. Contemporary Teacher predictions for 24 respondents per city separate discrepancies already present in the Teacher from those that arise between Student and Teacher. In a 1,000-traveler Helsinki simulation, feasibility-constrained assignment makes more trips routable without narrowing the gap between predicted and simulated responses. Fidelity at one level therefore does not carry over to the next, and the choice of traveler model should follow the intervention and behavioral outcome that a simulation is meant to inform.
